--TEST--
Core\Time::at refuses a calendar field outside its own range where mktime() rolls it over into the next month, day or hour
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
`mktime`/`gmmktime` accept any integer in any of their six positional slots and *normalize* the result: `gmmktime(0, 0, 0, 13, 1, 2024)` is 2025-01-01, `gmmktime(0, 0, 0, 2, 30, 2024)` is 2024-03-01, a `0` month or day steps backwards into the previous one, and hour `25` is 01:00 the next day. Every one of those is a real date that the caller did not write, arrived at by arithmetic PHP performs silently — and the value that reaches the caller is a `time_t` with nothing on it to say a field was out of range, so the mistake surfaces one report or one billing period later. `Core\Time::at` has no such answer to give. Its parameters name calendar fields, so a `month` of `13` names no month at all and there is no month for the call to be *about*; it is a `Fault::thrown` naming the member and the field (`nvs_stdlib::time`'s `out_of_range`), catchable like any other `Throwable` (ADR 0002), and the caller who meant "thirteen months after January" writes it as the calendar arithmetic it is — `Core\Time::at(2024, 1, 1, $zone)->plus(12, Core\Unit::Month)`, the first row below, which is `at`'s own agreement with `mktime`'s rolled-over answer where that answer was intended. This is AGENTS.md's priority ordering at 2 over 4: a silently normalized date is a wrong answer that runs, and the throw is the same trade ADR 0007 § 2 makes for every conversion. Two neighbouring bounds are not observable here at all and are named rather than left implied. A **negative** month, day or hour is refused by the checker before the program exists, `at`'s three fields being `uint`, where `mktime(-1, …)` rolls over like any other value — the same run-time-throw-into-compile-time-refusal `Core\Arr::fill`'s own divergence case describes. And a **year** outside `-9999..=9999` throws here where PHP's 64-bit `time_t` carries it, which is the range spec § 4's civil calendar is defined over; it is the last row below, and it is the one refusal whose field PHP never rolls over at all. The agreeing half — every date both implementations accept, the two civil times New York's DST transitions make ambiguous among them — is asserted against PHP itself in `time-at-matches-mktime-and-gmmktime`.
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Refusal {
    // A refusal is read as *this member's* own wording rather than as the
    // calendar library's: `out_of_range` prefixes every one of them with the
    // member that made it, and what the row below asserts is that the call was
    // refused there and named itself, not how the range was worded.
    public static function of(string $message): string {
        return Core\Str::startsWith($message, "Core\\Time::at(): ") as string;
    }
}

var $utc = Core\Time\Zone::UTC;
uint $refused = 0;

// What `gmmktime(0, 0, 0, 13, 1, 2024)` meant, written as the calendar
// arithmetic it is. This row agrees with PHP's rolled-over answer exactly —
// which is what makes the refusals below a demand for the spelling rather than
// a capability Novis lacks.
echo Core\Time::at(2024, 1, 1, $utc)->plus(12, Core\Unit::Month)->format("yyyy-MM-dd"), "\n";
echo Core\Time::at(2024, 2, 1, $utc)->plus(29, Core\Unit::Day)->format("yyyy-MM-dd"), "\n";

// A month past December, and the `0` PHP steps backwards from.
try { Core\Time::at(2024, 13, 1, $utc); } catch (Throwable $e) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo "month=13|", Refusal::of($e->message), "\n"; }
try { Core\Time::at(2024, 0, 1, $utc); } catch (Throwable $m0) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo "month=0|", Refusal::of($m0->message), "\n"; }

// A day the month does not have, and the `0` PHP reads as the last day of the
// month before. The first is the date that does not exist at all: 2024 is a
// leap year, so February has 29 days and this is the first day it has not.
try { Core\Time::at(2024, 2, 30, $utc); } catch (Throwable $d30) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo "feb=30|", Refusal::of($d30->message), "\n"; }
try { Core\Time::at(2024, 3, 0, $utc); } catch (Throwable $d0) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo "day=0|", Refusal::of($d0->message), "\n"; }

// An option out of range is refused on the same terms as a parameter: the bag
// is a spelling of the same six fields, not a laxer way in.
try { Core\Time::at(2024, 3, 1, $utc, {hour: 25}); } catch (Throwable $h25) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo "hour=25|", Refusal::of($h25->message), "\n"; }
try { Core\Time::at(2024, 3, 1, $utc, {minute: 60}); } catch (Throwable $mi) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo "minute=60|", Refusal::of($mi->message), "\n"; }

// Past the width the field is carried in, rather than past the calendar's own
// range — one field, one refusal, whichever bound it crossed.
try { Core\Time::at(2024, 300, 1, $utc); } catch (Throwable $m300) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo "month=300|", Refusal::of($m300->message), "\n"; }

// The year, which PHP does not roll over and does not refuse either.
try { Core\Time::at(10000, 1, 1, $utc); } catch (Throwable $y) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo "year=10000|", Refusal::of($y->message), "\n"; }

// Counted rather than read off the lines above: a field that stopped being
// refused fails here as well as shifting its own row.
echo "refused=", $refused, "/8\n";
--EXPECT--
2025-01-01
2024-03-01
month=13|1
month=0|1
feb=30|1
day=0|1
hour=25|1
minute=60|1
month=300|1
year=10000|1
refused=8/8
